c GCD of $x^3-x$ and $x^2-1$.","options":["$x^2-1$","$1$","$x$","$x-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d1079209200059c244f0b79f","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_003_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Long division gives $x^3-1=x\\,(x^2-1)+(x-1)$. Which pair has exactly the same common divisors as the pair $(x^3-1,\\,x^2-1)$?","options":["$(x^2-1,\\ x-1)$","$(x,\\ x-1)$","$(x^3-1,\\ x)$","$(x^2-1,\\ x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"796cab4015220f078135527c","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_016_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The Euclidean algorithm applied to $a$ and $b$ produces the remainders $x^2-1$, then $2x-2$, then $0$. What is the monic GCD of $a$ and $b$?","options":["$0$","$x-1$","$x^2-1$","$2x-2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b1f6c16938dd8c756450c922","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_017_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must the Euclidean algorithm in $\\mathbb{F}[x]$ stop after finitely many steps?","options":["The degrees of the quotients strictly decrease","The degrees of the nonzero remainders strictly decrease","The coefficients of the remainders shrink in absolute value","Each step removes a root shared by the two polynomials"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3d5a0ab14384d97ec2211a32","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_018_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Over $\\mathbb{R}$, let $a(x)=x^3+x$ and $b(x)=x^2+1$. Which statement is correct?","options":["Their monic GCD is $x^2+1$, and they have no common real root","They have no common real root, so their monic GCD is $1$","Their monic GCD is $x^2+1$, so they have a common real root","Their monic GCD is $x$, the factor giving the root $0$ of $a$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fa3117efac4d9c9482882a94","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch21_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_019_curated20261027_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Over $\\mathbb{R}$, what is the monic GCD of $x^3-2x^2+x$ and $x^2-1$?","options":["$x-1$","$(x-1)^2$","$x(x-1)$","$x^2-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2587cb85ee5369eee9565c3e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_polynomial_greatest_common_divisor_8189e46b_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $a(x),b(x)\\in\\mathbb{R}[x]$, not both zero, let $g(x)$ be a GCD of $a$ and $b$, and let $d(x)$ be any common divisor of $a$ and $b$. Which statement must hold?","options":["$\\deg d<\\deg g$","$g$ divides $d$","$d$ is a constant multiple of $g$","$d$ divides $g$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"111d292764981a9edaa57551","time_limit":35}]}}